T7 T8: T7 T7 pair, T4 T8 need T5 or T6 or T9 to match \u2014 actually messy.\n\nBetter to think in terms of needed draws:  \nWe need T2, T3, T5, T6, T8, T9 to complete chows/pungs.  \nKeeping T5 is useful (T5 T6 T7 chow possible if we get T7).  \nKeeping T8 is useful (T6 T7 T8 possible if we get T6).  \n\nMost useless in hand for Full Flush:  \nJ3 (obvious), then B5 (not Bamboo), then among Bamboos:  \nWhich Bamboo tile is worst?  \nT4 we have 2 copies \u2014 can be pair or part of chow.  \nT5 only 1 copy, but T5 is central for many chows.  \nT6 only 1 copy, also central.  \nT7 two copies \u2014 can be pair.  \nT8 one copy \u2014 less useful if we can\u2019t get T6 T7 T8.  \n\nRight now worst Bamboo might be T8 because T6 is only 1, T8 alone without T6 T7 or T9?  \nBut we have T7s, so if we draw T6, T8 becomes useful.  \n\nActually, safest discard now is J3 \u2014 no risk, keeps all options open.\n\n**Valid actions:** Play B5, Play J3, Play T6, Play T4, Play T8, Play T7, Play T5.\n\nPlay J3: safe, no effect on Bamboo flush plan.  \nPlay T8: reduces flush potential slightly but keeps B5 for later discard.  \nPlay B5: risky, could give others pung.  \n\nGiven the safe discard J3, no downside.\n\nEvaluation: Discard J3 is safe, keeps Full Flush possibility intact, avoids giving others points.  \nReason: J3 is completely safe (3 already discarded), preserves all Bamboo tiles for potential high-scoring hand.\n\n**Final choice:** Play J3.\n\n**Answer:** Play J3"}
